{"vuvuzela":{"id":"04e0aa21-7d38-412f-97e1-e77e876cff35","word":"vuvuzela","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"horn","tags":["feminine"],"word":"vuvuzela"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"When the Manchester United squad walked through the arrival gates at Durban International yesterday, most players heard a vuvuzela for the first time.","ref":"2006 July 15, Weekend Argus:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[122,130]],"id":"a44fe176-0af9-4faa-af7d-6ec053258e17"},{"text":"I’m talking, of course, about the vuvuzela. Essentially, a metre-long blowhorn, which you may struggle to get past a steward at an English football ground.","ref":"2010 June 1, David Pickthall, “Are you ready to make some noise?”, in North-West Evening Mail, UK, retrieved 02 Aug 2011:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[34,42]],"id":"1f0e7dd7-f6d8-4d85-8579-171631d5bfe8"},{"text":"For some, the vuvuzelas herald the country’s piercing emergence from the forced racial hush of apartheid.","ref":"2010 August 6, Jeré Longman, “A Bleating Soundtrack for Soccer”, in The New York Times:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[14,23]],"id":"75b1dff5-26a3-4d6b-b74b-6c10b2aef148"}],"links":[["horn","horn"]],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms borrowed from Zulu","English terms derived from Zulu","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 14 entries","Pages with entries","Terms with Afrikaans translations","Terms with Arabic translations","Terms with Armenian translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Esperanto translations","Terms with Faroese translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Hebrew translations","Terms w